As a real-time critic of just about every single one of Waller's recent speeches that have ended with a huge egg on his face, this may be the worst one yet. On the plus side, he finally got the data piece right, but he has ramped up the hawkish tone into what is likely to be the very pico top of inflation (cyclically). In a few months time, the forward guidance he delivered in today's speech will look extremely silly. Both inflation and growth have peaked and it couldn't be more classic monetary policy to be making decisions that operate with "long and variable lags" while staring through the rearview mirror. On a multi-week to multi-month time horizon, this is a fade.

